          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "**What a combination code is**\n\nA combination code puts two conditions, or a condition and a complication, into one code. The reference explains that where a combination code exists, it is used instead of separate codes. This is why some families, such as diabetes, contain so many codes. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\n**Conditions in this reference that use combination codes**\n\n- **Diabetes (E11 family).** The combination code names the link between type 2 diabetes and a complication. Some complications also need a second code for detail. For example, diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for the stage of kidney disease, and a diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for its site and depth. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- **Hypertension (I11, I12, I13).** When hypertension affects an organ, the classification prefers a combination code over I10, which is used only when hypertension stands alone:\n  - I11 is hypertensive heart disease. If heart failure is present, its type is added from I50 as a second code.\n  - I12 is hypertension with chronic kidney disease. A second code gives the stage of kidney disease.\n  - I13 is used when both heart and kidney involvement are documented.\n\n  (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\n**Related rules**\n\n- The word \"with\" in the classification index is read as \"associated with\" or \"due to.\" The relationship is presumed unless the record says the conditions are unrelated. For example, type 2 diabetes listed with chronic kidney disease is coded as diabetic kidney disease by default. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes*.)\n- I50 (heart failure) is not a combination code. Its codes are added to a combination code, not used in place of it. (Source: *Coding heart failure: I50*.)\n\n**What the reference does not cover**\n\nThe retrieved passages do not say whether I15 (secondary hypertension) or I16 (hypertensive crisis) are combination codes, so I can't say that here. Code assignments also change over time, so check any specific code against a current official code file or with a qualified coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
